related_terms: defense-in-depth
quick_facts: ≈440 power reactors worldwide — ~10% of global electricity
quick_facts: Fuel: 3–5% enriched uranium — far below weapons grade
quick_facts: Control rods, moderator, and coolant keep k exactly 1
quick_facts: Also built for ships, research, and medical isotopes
quick_facts: Life-cycle carbon comparable to wind power

sources:
    {"organization":"IAEA","title":"Nuclear Power Reactors","url":"https://www.iaea.org/topics/nuclear-power-reactors"}

definition:
    A machine holding a nuclear chain reaction at exactly critical — steady — power. Fuel assemblies of enriched uranium sit in a lattice with moderator and coolant while control rods trim reactivity; heat from fission boils water or gas to spin turbines. Around 440 power reactors supply roughly a tenth of world electricity with near-zero carbon, and specialized designs produce medical isotopes, research beams, and naval propulsion. Physics itself leans safe: most designs shut down if cooling or moderation is lost.

debunking_snippet:
    “A reactor can detonate like an atomic bomb.” Physically impossible — bomb fuel is >90% enriched and assembled supercritically in microseconds; reactor fuel is dilute ceramic that can't sustain that.
